WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.313 --> 00:00:02.970
- Julia?

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.803
Julia!

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.360
Julia, what are you doing?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.290
- Oh, well, I've been binging
old fixer upper episodes

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.950
and I decided this ugly back
splash simply has to go.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:14.790
Oh, and careful.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.180
Don't trip over that tasteful
mid-century lighting fixture.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.220
Oh, and don't touch those walls.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.750
They're freshly painted.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.550
Uber neutral Beige.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.780
- Okay. I'm not gonna lie,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.000
I'm kind of loving the whole Airbnb feel,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.130
but how much is it gonna cost?

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.000
- Well don't worry, babe.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.060
It'll all end up back in our
pockets at the end of the day.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.820
Investing in equity. Am I right?

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.670
Maybe I could even become a house flipper.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.500
- Okay, I think this has
gone a little too far.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.800
Not all remodels end
up like they do on TV.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.750
And how about we just do
a little bit of digging

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.301
to see what's really
worth the time and effort?

00:00:48.301 --> 00:00:50.884
(upbeat music)

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.610
- Home improvement is big business.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.960
Globally, the industry is
worth about $333 billion

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.220
and it's expected to grow
to over 500 billion by 2028.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.280
The pandemic actually
facilitated this growth.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.530
That kitchen you used to forego using,

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.040
in order to eat out became
your new sourdough baking zone.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.220
Staring at that ugly
wall color all day long,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.020
finally became too much to bear.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.380
And with dining out and travel
taking up less of our budgets

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.740
home projects happily
gobbled up our free dollars.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.090
- Working on your living space

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.100
has also become entertainment.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.010
I remember watching the
OG home improvement show,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.980
This Old House on PBS,
as a kid with my parents.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.800
And these days, there are
entire networks devoted

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.490
to property improvement
like HGTV and Magnolia.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.950
Designers and developers,
like Chip and Joanna Gaines

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.650
and the Property Brothers,
are literal celebrities

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.270
and before and after footage

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.310
doesn't just show up on the TV anymore.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.920
Home renovations and DIY influencers

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.260
are also dominating our social feeds.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.730
- Now, I get it.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:03.930
Seeing before and after
pictures is like visual ASMR,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.120
but if you've ever
attempted a home project,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.760
beyond just replacing a broken appliance,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.090
you know it can get eye
wateringly expensive.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.160
In fact, for about the last decade,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.190
home improvement costs have
been steadily climbing,

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.520
above and beyond national
baseline inflation.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.920
Covid and the ensuing spike in prices

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.200
obviously made it a lot worse,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.200
but the average cost versus value ratio

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.560
hasn't been looking
pretty since around 2014.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.480
So what sort of improvements

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.090
really give you the
most bang for your buck?

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.620
And if you're a renter,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.930
is there anything you can
do to improve your place

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.460
without breaking the bank?

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.220
- Okay, Julia, wanna play a
little home improvement game?

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.080
- Oh, heck yeah, I got this.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.150
- What do you think is the most profitable

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.740
home improvement you can make?

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.640
- Oh, kitchen. Oh, no, no, wait. Bathroom.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.473
Final answer.

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:55.830
- *buzz* sorry.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.780
Topping the list with the
most bang for your buck

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.660
is garage door replacement.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.140
In 2021, people on average
spent about four grand

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.480
to replace a garage door, and upon resale,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.180
got about $3,700 back.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.870
That means you're losing 7
cents on every dollar invested,

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.570
which is about the best
you can expect these days.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.100
- The Journal Of Light Construction

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.620
has been tracking national
and regional costs

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.830
for home improvement
projects for 35 years.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.410
They then compare the cost to the value

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.050
of the improvement upon
the sale of the house.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.140
- Obviously, ROI can
depend on your location.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.360
An improvement in one area of the country

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.580
might not be as valuable as in another.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.500
- Turns out, according to their study,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.910
kitchen remodeling, my favorite,
comes in at number three

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.450
in terms of profitability
with 71 cents on the dollar,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.120
and bathroom redos are even worse,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.790
usually between 50 and 60 cents.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.690
Of the top 12 projects
that give the highest ROI,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.230
11 of them are actually
exterior focused projects,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:02.040
like upgrading siding and
adding veneer accents,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.870
which actually makes sense.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.420
People tend to customize indoor spaces

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.240
more to their own liking
and personal taste,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.270
which might not translate
to the next buyer.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.430
But when it comes to outside projects,

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.550
you're forced to consider what
strangers might be thinking

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.530
as they walk or drive by.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:20.700
- It also might surprise you,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.850
that things actually get worse
the more money you put in.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.830
Many studies looking at remodeling numbers

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.620
tend to break up projects by budget level.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.590
On one side, you have
minor cosmetic improvements

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.630
and on the other you have major ones,

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.990
like knocking down walls and
putting in upscale features.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.700
According to Remodeling Magazine,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.030
the average cost of a
minor kitchen facelift

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.840
will run you about $14,773.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.630
If you turn that around and
sold that home a year later,

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.540
you'd get about 87% of that back.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.580
But if you went for the mid-range do over

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.770
and spent close to $40,000,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.800
that would go down to about 80% back.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.380
Wanna splurge and spend $80,000?

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.860
You might only get close to
half back of what you put in.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.200
Also, remember that these
numbers aren't assuming

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.470
that you're paying interest
or taking out debt to do it.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.140
- However, not all is financially lost,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.660
if you wanna make some
improvements to your space.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.920
Here are a few tricks

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.160
that can help you remain as
in the black as possible.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.650
First off, you're already engaging

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:27.030
with the most incredible tool
for home improvement, YouTube.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.460
Learning how to cut down on labor costs

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.500
is a huge way to save.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.710
Skills like demoing, painting, tiling,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.420
and refinishing surfaces,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.600
can all be done relatively
easily by amateurs.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.550
If you're looking to cut energy costs

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.140
with energy efficient upgrades,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.170
like replacing a water heater, HVAC unit,

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.750
or even solar panels,
you can take advantage

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.760
of some pretty sweet tax credits

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.800
due to the Inflation Reduction Act

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.870
that can really help your bottom line.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.160
- But what about renters?

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.970
I mean, they make up about 36%
of the American population.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.920
Believe it or not, there's
a lot of cool stuff

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.690
that you can do to your place as a renter,

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.020
that doesn't mean you'll be
lining your landlord's pockets.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.390
Most leases will allow
you to change out things,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:12.180
like lighting, fixtures,
faucets, shower heads,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.550
even changing out drawer handle hardware

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.980
can be easy and make a big difference.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.960
Just make sure you keep
the originals handy

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.280
for when it's time to move.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.770
But once you've bought them
yourself, they're yours to keep.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.780
- Changing the walls and flooring

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.030
can make a huge difference in a space.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.730
But don't be tempted to rip up that carpet

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.850
or linoleum or paint those
rented walls just yet.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.460
Instead, invest in things like rugs,

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.580
that can be easily moved
from one place to another.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.300
There are also lots of really
cool stick and peel solutions,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.850
for things like walls and back splashes,

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.410
and even bathroom floors,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.260
to cover up those unsightly patterns.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.330
- Okay, Jules, how are you feeling?

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.340
- I suppose I can acknowledge

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.230
that replacing this back splash

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.510
is more like replacing a pair of pants

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.870
I don't really like anymore,
than investing in my IRA.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.850
- Exactly, most experts agree

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.700
home renovations aren't
really investments.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.350
Who would wanna invest in something

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.130
that has a close to guaranteed
negative rate of return?

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.920
But that doesn't mean you
shouldn't attempt to do them.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:14.790
Just approach it

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.520
like any other lifestyle
upgrade you're considering.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.220
Don't take out debt, yank
from your emergency fund,

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.320
or touch your investments to do it.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.920
- Research, a little sweat
equity, and strategic splurges

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.410
will keep you sitting financially pretty

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.560
through those before and after pictures.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.390
- And that's our 2 cents.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:34.950
- Before you go, we wanna tell you about

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.660
a new slate of documentary series

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.970
on our sister channel PBS Voices.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:41.640
- These series cover everything
from hip hop and tech,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.470
to the origins of sacred rituals,

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.390
unconventional community engagement,

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.660
and the welcoming spaces
in the LGBTQ community.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.070
- There's something for everyone.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.200
So if you haven't been over to PBS Voices,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.520
we hope you check them out.

